From: genshanxinli <127909456+genshanxinli@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:20:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: reserve per-band token quota

The token pool is global: mt76_token_consume() blocks every band once
dev->token_count reaches token_size - MT76_TOKEN_FREE_THR. A 4SS 6 GHz
burst can therefore occupy the whole pool and leave the 5 GHz and 2.4 GHz
bands without a token to send from, even though those bands only need a
handful.

Account the in-flight tokens per band and add token_reserved[band], a
configurable reservation (default 0). While another band has traffic in
flight, or scheduled or queued a frame within the last 500 ms, its
reservation is subtracted from the pool this band may use, so a burst
cannot take the reserved entries. A band whose remaining share would fall
inside the free headroom keeps the shared pool instead, so a band can
always leave the blocked state on the next completion. An increase must
leave two free thresholds in the pool; a decrease or clearing is always
accepted, so a reservation can be backed out after the pool shrank.

The blocked state is tracked per band instead of being set and cleared
for all bands together, so the band that reached its ceiling waits for
completions while a band that still has pool entries keeps scheduling. A
reservation is released as soon as its band goes idle, so a single active
band still uses the whole pool. The driver's unblock request clears the
per-band flags and always reschedules the TX worker in quota mode, because
the release path may already have cleared the flag that made progress; a
release without a wake pointer, such as a failed TX preparation returning
its token, refreshes and reschedules on its own.

The per-band state is a separate flag and not the shared q->blocked:
mt7996_register_phy() aliases the 6 GHz queues to the band1 q_tx[0] when
the NPU is active (mt7996/init.c:761-769), so band1 and band2 feed one DMA
ring and a single q->blocked could not express "band1 runs while band2 is
blocked" - the two bands used to clear each other's bit. mt76_txq_stopped()
therefore takes the phy and, while a quota is configured, reads
token_band_stopped[phy->band_idx] instead. The ring itself stays shared,
so a full ring still stops both bands, which no software split can avoid;
the debugfs file says so rather than implying a per-band queue.

With no reservation configured the original __mt76_set_tx_blocked() body
runs verbatim, so the default keeps the global threshold and the old
set/clear behavior; the per-band state and blocked counter are only
maintained while the quota is on.

Expose a 0600 debugfs file "token_quota" next to "token_info" with the
per-band used/reserved/blocked counters and the current per-band block
state:

  band |   used | reserved | blocked | state
     0 |     12 |        0 |       0 | run
     1 |      8 |      512 |       3 | stop
     2 |   8000 |        0 |     127 | run

  pool: 8020 of 16384 in flight, quota on
  txq: band1 and band2 share one TX ring, so a full ring stops both;
       only the token block is tracked (and cleared) per band

The "txq:" lines are computed from the live q_tx[0] pointers, so they
appear exactly when the NPU aliasing is in effect.

Writing "<band> <tokens>" sets one reservation, e.g. "echo '1 512' >
token_quota"; writing 0 to every band returns to the default. The
counters and the reset cleanup are maintained under token_lock, and no
AQL, firmware or pool size is changed.

The patch applies at -F0 after 0001..0017-notify-the-NPU-when-a-station-
is-removed and builds mt76.ko, mt76-connac-lib.ko, mt7996e.ko (NPU
config) and mt7925-common.ko against the target 7.3 kernel tree. The
5 GHz blocked-counter A/B on hardware is left for a follow-up, as the
issue says. The 5 GHz/6 GHz ring sharing is documented, not removed:
splitting the ring needs a firmware and ring-layout change, not a quota.

Signed-off-by: genshanxinli <127909456+genshanxinli@users.noreply.github.com>
